      "title": "Timurid Empire: Conquest, Courts and Persian Culture | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Timurid Iran through Timur’s conquests, the courts of Samarkand and Herat, Persian historical writing, and surviving architectural heritage."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "empire-and-geography",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An empire across modern borders",
        "content": "The Timurid story begins beyond the borders of present-day Iran. In 1370, Timur, known in English as Tamerlane, established his power in Transoxiana, the Central Asian region associated with Samarkand. From this capital he conducted campaigns across Iran and neighboring lands. His dynasty therefore belongs to several interconnected histories rather than exclusively to one modern country. [s1]\n\nTimur’s conquests reached far beyond the territory his descendants could retain. His death in 1405 exposed the difficulty of holding this enormous realm together. Shah Rukh subsequently became its dominant ruler, but competition among Timurid princes gradually reduced the dynasty’s territorial reach. Its later centers lay in Khorasan, the historical northeastern Iranian region extending into present-day Afghanistan, and Transoxiana. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "conquest-and-consequences",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The violence behind imperial prestige",
        "content": "Timurid cultural achievement should be read alongside the human consequences of conquest. At Isfahan in 1387, resistance involving the killing of Timur’s tax collectors was followed by a massacre. Historical accounts describe catastrophic destruction, but their casualty totals are not equivalent to a modern population count. No precise death toll is adopted here. [s2]\n\nThis local history complicates any simple account of a uniformly flourishing age. A court could accumulate skilled workers and commission extraordinary objects while communities elsewhere experienced killing, displacement, and weakened urban life. Isfahan’s experience makes the distinction between imperial prestige and the security of subjects particularly clear. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "courts-and-books",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Herat and the written world",
        "content": "Herat, now in Afghanistan, became a major center of Timurid patronage. Court libraries supported the preparation of elaborate manuscripts, bringing writing, calligraphy, illumination, and painting into a shared enterprise. The late court of Sultan Husayn Bayqara was especially important to this artistic flowering. [s1]\n\nHistorical writing also expanded. Persian was its predominant language, while Chaghatay, a literary Turkic language, gained importance within the same intellectual environment. Authors produced dynastic chronicles, local histories, biographies, and guides to shrines. These genres show that Timurid intellectual life extended beyond the celebration of individual rulers. [s3]\n\nNevertheless, patronage shaped the evidence that survives. Official historians worked to establish Timur’s legitimacy and justify the claims of particular successors. Their narratives require comparison rather than automatic acceptance: a chronicle can preserve valuable testimony while also advancing its patron’s political position. The names, genealogies, and succession stories in these works were arguments about authority as well as records of events. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "architectural-afterlife",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A legacy larger than the dynasty",
        "content": "Samarkand’s surviving ensembles provide another way to approach the period. UNESCO identifies the Bibi-Khanum Mosque, Gur-Emir ensemble, and remains of Ulugh Beg’s observatory among its major monuments. These sites connect imperial commemoration with religious architecture and scientific activity. [s4]\n\nTheir influence crossed later political boundaries. UNESCO traces the contribution of Timurid architecture to subsequent developments under the Safavids in Iran, the Mughals in India, and the Ottomans. The most useful conclusion is therefore regional: Timurid political unity proved fragile, while its artistic and intellectual models remained available to successor societies. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
